00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:14.830
It’s now 2019 and everything is getting
old.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:20.340
Ellen Degeneres is now sixty, Comments on
why I’m wrong about Florida’s flag are

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:26.230
getting ancient, not that I read those, or
anything, and millennials are now officially

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.380
full-grown adults.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.470
The youngest are graduating college and entering
the job market.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:35.240
The oldest, are well into their careers.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:40.620
And while some companies are busy complaining
about millennials killing their business,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:47.230
others are realizing Hey, this generation
might not be exactly the same as their parents,

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.740
and are adapting.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:49.860
Imagine that.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:56.180
Hence: Lots of bright colors, artsy fonts,
emojis, and manufactured authenticity.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.710
Prolly not a great time to announce the new
PolyMatter branding…

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.730
One of the most successful of these companies
is called WeWork.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:09.200
In case you’re not a 20-35 year-old who
lives here and likes to work from a beanbag,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.880
(no judgement), here’s the idea:

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:17.210
Instead of leasing an office for a whole year,
or trying to write a script about this hot

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:23.280
new startup called WeWork in a Starbucks while
the barista yells “Karen!” for the 7th

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.040
time in 2 minutes,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.310
You can
rent a space in one of WeWork’s 500 buildings

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.240
in 97 cities.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.270
For somewhere between two and eight hundred
dollars a month, you get a private office,

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:38.650
a desk, or the opportunity of a desk.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.280
They provide the Wifi, furniture, printers,
and cleaning.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:48.460
It’s got all the ingredients of a fun, exciting,
young startup - craft beer, pictures of people

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.220
doing yoga, and chairs that don’t look like
chairs.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:57.750
It may not sound revolutionary but it’s
perfectly timed with the rise of independent,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:03.780
remote, and freelance workers, who there are
now 68 million of in the U.S. alone.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:08.630
There just one problem, or a few billion,
actually.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:14.480
WeWork is the 4th highest valued startup in
the world, just behind Uber and its Chinese

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.640
competitor.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.140
Many have called it massively overvalued,
even the most overvalued.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:25.800
Meanwhile, it’s starting a school, called
WeGrow, a gym called “Rise By We”, and

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:31.459
renting apartments called WeLive, Which all
sounds a we bit… scattershot.

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:33.470
So, what’s the deal?

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:40.480
The answer is just as interesting as what
it says about… our whole economy.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.350
The way WeWork makes money, or tries to, is
actually pretty simple.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:51.470
It is starting to buy a few of its own buildings,
but for the most part, here’s how it works:

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:58.190
First, it finds big, centrally-located buildings
in young, densely populated areas and signs

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.260
a five, ten, even fifteen-year lease.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:06.690
Which makes the landlord really happy, at
least, for now.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:12.250
And then, they turn around and sublease to
you and I, on a monthly basis.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:13.629
Pretty simple, right?

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:16.880
A little too simple, you might be thinking.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.250
If WeWork can make money appear out of thin
air by splitting a big lease into a bunch

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.370
of tiny ones, why doesn’t the building just
do that itself?

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.620
Isn’t WeWork just an inefficient middleman?

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.750
Instead of paying one landlord, now we’re
essentially paying two.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:39.370
And that’s true - nothing stops the owner
of a building from creating its own shared

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:40.560
offices.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:41.680
Some do.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.879
But it’s not so simple.

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:45.319
For two reasons.

00:03:45.319 --> 00:03:50.750
First, WeWork and the owner are really playing
two, very different games.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:58.000
At its core, WeWork is one, giant, $47 billion
bet that its real estate will increase in

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.000
value.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.700
Because if you and I pay more every month
for a desk, WeWork still pays the same 10,

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.959
15, whatever-year lease, and they pocket the
difference.

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:14.880
On the other hand, if can I say, when? property
values fall, they have to pay the difference.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.219
Now, each lease is under a different subsidiary,
so if it can’t afford to make payments,

00:04:20.219 --> 00:04:23.439
the company, as a whole, is somewhat protected.

00:04:23.439 --> 00:04:29.520
But if a bunch of them fail at the same time,
like during a recession, it could be really,

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:30.839
really bad.

00:04:30.839 --> 00:04:36.219
And remember that most of WeWork’s customers
are small startups and freelancers, ya know,

00:04:36.219 --> 00:04:40.330
the businesses most likely to fail during
a recession.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:41.330
Oops.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:46.749
The owner, on the other hand, gets a consistent,
arguably lower-risk paycheck and makes its

00:04:46.749 --> 00:04:49.610
building more valuable in the meantime.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:54.110
The second reason is that WeWork has an unfair
advantage.

00:04:54.110 --> 00:05:00.849
Unlike the property owner, it can set prices
unreasonably low, attracting far more customers.

00:05:00.849 --> 00:05:05.889
The secret is that WeWork is always on sale
- you just don’t know it.

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:09.180
How can it sell a service for less than what
it costs?

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.569
The answer is a little, actually, remarkably
not-so-little, company called SoftBank.

00:05:14.569 --> 00:05:19.580
You’ve never heard of it because it’s
a multinational holding conglomerate.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.379
In English, a company that owns companies.

00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:24.479
A big pile of money.

00:05:24.479 --> 00:05:31.779
Softbank’s Vision Fund is nearly $100 billion,
provided by Saudi Arabia, the United Arab

00:05:31.779 --> 00:05:35.069
Emirates, and companies like Apple and Qualcomm.

00:05:35.069 --> 00:05:36.849
Best friends, I hear.

00:05:36.849 --> 00:05:41.840
To put that in perspective, because, frankly,
anything after like 6 zeros(?) tends to look

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:42.840
the same,

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.029
These are the other biggest venture capital
firms.

00:05:46.029 --> 00:05:51.580
The Vision Fund makes even Goldman Sachs look
more… bronze than gold.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.229
So what does it do with all that money?

00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:56.399
Mostly, invest in tech companies.

00:05:56.399 --> 00:06:00.969
And, when those run out, anything that remotely
looks like one.

00:06:00.969 --> 00:06:06.729
Basically, add “big data” or “blockchain”
to your name and then buy a bigger wallet,

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:09.289
because Softbank wants all of it.

00:06:09.289 --> 00:06:14.210
Just a few weeks ago, it poured another $2
billion into WeWork.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:19.659
And if you think that’s a lot, which, it
is, it was originally going to be sixteen

00:06:19.659 --> 00:06:20.659
billion.

00:06:20.659 --> 00:06:25.219
So, Softbank, in a round-a-bout way, is paying
for part of your office, in hopes that the

00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:28.750
company will grow and one day make a profit.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:35.419
Which is all too common - Enjoy those $1 scooters,
and cheap Uber rides because some venture

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:37.610
capitalist somewhere is paying for them.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:43.129
It won’t last forever, just ask anyone with
Moviepass how that turned out.

00:06:43.129 --> 00:06:44.169
But hang on.

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:49.310
Why does Softbank, who likes tech companies
like Uber and Bytedance, invest in WeWork,

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:50.689
who leases real estate?

00:06:50.689 --> 00:06:56.050
Well, that’s the question - is WeWork a
tech company, or does it just really want

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:57.050
to be?

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:00.959
Because, if it’s just a middleman between
property owners and renters,

00:07:00.959 --> 00:07:04.939
and Uber just a platform connecting drivers
and riders,

00:07:04.939 --> 00:07:10.610
both of which use technology but suffer the
economics of a non-tech company,

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.180
then they’re a lot less exciting.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:18.300
If that’s true, a lot of investors are spending
a lot more than they should, and when they

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.740
realize this, things are going to get ugly.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:27.520
Think about it this way: A tech company can
potentially have seven billion users instantly.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.669
A thousand downloads don’t cost any more
than one.

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:34.180
Scale is (nearly) unlimited.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.520
And therefore, so are valuations.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:42.650
WeWork, however, will always have to buy or
lease more space as more people join.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.740
It scales, but not in the same way.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:51.719
To put it in perspective, there’s this other,
less exciting, co-working company called Regus.

00:07:51.719 --> 00:07:57.860
This is its total square feet, and this is
WeWork’s, again, with a valuation of $47

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:58.860
billion.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.739
So what’s Regus valued at?

00:08:00.739 --> 00:08:03.970
4 Billion - That’s not with a zero.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:04.970
Hmmm…

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:08.789
Oh yeah and, fun fact: Regus actually makes
a profit.

00:08:08.789 --> 00:08:10.199
I know right?

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:11.430
Who does that?

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:13.319
So old fashioned!

00:08:13.319 --> 00:08:17.909
The question is: What makes WeWork worth that
extra 43 billion?

00:08:17.909 --> 00:08:20.589
Why is it valued like a tech company?

00:08:20.589 --> 00:08:24.030
And Regus, based on, ya know, how much money
it can make?

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:26.369
Now, it’s tempting to stop here.

00:08:26.369 --> 00:08:31.759
To say WeWork is just a real estate company
and yes, it’s overvalued.

00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:34.129
But, that’s not totally fair.

00:08:34.129 --> 00:08:39.589
There’s a reason WeWork is so much more
successful and it’s not just that Softbank

00:08:39.589 --> 00:08:41.529
thinks its money is a hot potato,

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:45.589
It’s because WeWork has mastered the art
of telling a story.

00:08:45.589 --> 00:08:52.649
Regus has nice, professional photos and advertises
with generic phrases like “Stay productive”,

00:08:52.649 --> 00:08:55.400
But WeWork understands The Millennial.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:09:00.740
Big art pieces, craft beer, and so many mentions
of the word “community” that I’m afraid

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:04.130
to Control-F their website for fear of crashing
my browser.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:09.100
“WeWork… (is) a state of consciousness,
a generation of interconnected emotionally

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:11.050
intelligent entrepreneurs.”

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:16.690
Roll your eyes all you want, and trust me,
I have, but it works.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:21.860
And it does have useful data, on where people
work, when they’re most productive, and

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:25.940
so on, which they can use to redesign and
optimize buildings.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:27.960
That’s valuable information.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:32.550
It has to decide, for example, how many conference
rooms to build based on how much they’ll

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:33.550
be used.

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:38.610
That’s worth thousands of dollars a month,
because if it builds even one too many rooms,

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:42.420
its wasting space that could’ve been a desk,
and made more money.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.560
So, is it real estate or tech company?

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:46.560
Yes.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.829
But, make no mistake: its core business fundamentally
relies on property values.

00:09:51.829 --> 00:09:57.470
That’s true no matter how hard it tries
to distract us, “Hey look over here, we’re

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:59.060
not just a real estate company”,

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:05.959
We’re opening a high-end gym in New York,
we bought a wave-pool company, Seriously though,

00:10:05.959 --> 00:10:08.759
why?! and we’re starting an elementary school!

00:10:08.759 --> 00:10:17.050
Which, by the way, costs up to $42,000 a year,
a total of $388,000 from age 2 to 11.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:23.399
Although, to be fair, it’s no ordinary school
- “A field of super-elliptic objects forms

00:10:23.399 --> 00:10:28.279
a learning landscape that’s dense and rational
– yet free and fluid.”

00:10:28.279 --> 00:10:33.181
Whatever that means, it’s not enough to
save the company from the fate of Regus, who

00:10:33.181 --> 00:10:36.699
after the dot-com crash, filed for bankruptcy.

00:10:36.699 --> 00:10:42.079
To survive its first recession, WeWork will
have to change its strategy or significantly

00:10:42.079 --> 00:10:44.690
diversify its income.

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:49.150
Economic downturns are inevitable, you have
to protect yourself by spreading your eggs

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.850
across multiple baskets.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:56.660
Likewise, it’s only a matter of time before
the next big hack - and when it happens, you

00:10:56.660 --> 00:11:01.689
don’t wanna be the guy with the same or
similar passwords across all your accounts.

